00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:19,080
 Now when you are adding an action group that integrates with the bedrock agent, there are

98
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:21,120
 two things you need to keep in mind.

99
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:25,600
 First you have to define the action group type and then action group invocation.

100
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:27,840
 So let me first talk about action group invocation.

101
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:33,320
 So it's just the lambda function that you would write your logic in which will make that

102
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:36,360
 API call to the airline database.

103
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:40,920
 So either you can create a new lambda function or you can select an existing lambda function

104
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:44,720
 that you might have already written.

105
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:46,800
 So that's the action group invocation.

106
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:49,000
 The first is the action group type.

107
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:55,840
 Now before the agent executes this action group, it needs certain guidance on what will

108
00:07:55,840 --> 00:08:00,040
 be the input parameters that will be passed by the user.

109
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:05,360
 So in our case, maybe source destination, date of travel and name of the user.

110
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:10,760
 So we have to define that somewhere and what is the response, maybe a booking ID.

111
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:14,120
 So you define those input and output parameters.

112
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:18,320
 So there are agent and execute the task better.

113
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:20,920
 And this is what action group type is used for.

114
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:26,480
 So when you select define with function details, so you can just go down and here you

115
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:31,800
 see the parameters and here you just click on add parameters and add the parameters like

116
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:34,640
 what is the user input going to look like.

117
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:38,440
 So source destination name of the traveler and date.

118
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:40,080
 So you define those parameters.

119
00:08:40,080 --> 00:08:44,960
 So it's much simpler when you use define with functionality detail.

120
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:48,400
 Next, the other option that you have is use the APA schema.

121
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:53,240
 So when I just click on this APA schema and you have two options, you have action group schema

122
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:54,240
 here.

123
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:55,840
 So select an existing APA schema.

124
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:59,960
 So if you already written and it can be written simple YAML.

125
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:05,560
 So you already written that and you upload it on the S3 bucket or you can do it where

126
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:06,720
 inline schema editor.

127
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:11,040
 So if I just click on this and this is what it's going to look like.

128
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:16,040
 So it simply defines what are the input parameters that the user is going to provide.

129
00:09:16,040 --> 00:09:22,520
 And those input parameters then would be utilized within the lambda function and then the

